    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Color polymorphisms are suggested to play a role in social and environmental adaptation, speciation, and potentially climate change response. Polymorphism, the appearance of two or more clearly different morphs within a population, is an example of the immense variation of life on earth. Swordtail and platyfish in the genus Xiphophorus possess a wide variety of polymorphic pigmentation traits. Of particular interest are the macromelanophore patterns, several of which are known to be involved in an extensively-studied hybrid incompatibility that causes melanoma in swordtail fish hybrids. The subject of this research is a trait we call the “confetti caudal,” a novel, previously unstudied macromelanophore polymorphism found in some populations of X. variatus. Here, I use a genome wide association study (GWAS) and tracking of lab born individuals to investigate the development and genetic basis of the trait. I find that the xmrk oncogene is a likely candidate for producing confetti caudal pigmentation. The size and number of macromelanophore spots continues to expand in sexually mature fish, and possible spontaneous melanoma formation was observed in non-hybrid fish. This research introduces a new phenotype to the body of knowledge on color polymorphisms in swordtail fish, an important system for studying vertebrate pigmentation, selection, and speciation.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Haase Cox, S. and Schumer, M. (2024). The Genetic Basis and Development of a Novel Tailspot Pattern in Xiphophorus variatus. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/th605wy9174. https://doi.org/10.25740/th605wy9174.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
